# Code quality

- Use strict TypeScript everywhere; never weaken `tsconfig.base.json`.
- Keep shared types centralised in `shared/src/types/index.ts`. Client and server import
  from `@treasure-trap/shared` — never duplicate type definitions.
- Prefer readable game logic over clever abstractions. Round resolvers should read
  top-to-bottom like the rules of the game.
- Never add unnecessary complexity: no new dependencies, layers, or patterns unless a
  concrete current requirement demands them.
- Do not hardcode fragile magic values in game logic. Scoring, item odds, timing windows,
  and rarity tables live in `shared/src/config/*` as named constants.
- Pure logic goes in `shared/src/game/` (no IO, no React, no sockets) so it can be unit
  tested. The server orchestrates; the shared modules decide.
- Clients send intents (`answer:submit`, `item:use`...); only the server mutates game state.
- Never expose `correctIndex` or other players' private state to clients during a question.
- Run `pnpm typecheck && pnpm lint && pnpm test` before finishing any change.
